    {
      "id": "bkHC",
      "code_hash": "af6f511a3bf2c96dda3b4e8bd9630edf",
      "outputs": [
        {
          "type": "data",
          "data": {
            "text/markdown": "<span class=\"markdown prose dark:prose-invert contents\"><h2 id=\"propagator-powers-and-the-auxiliary-mass\">Propagator powers and the auxiliary mass</h2>\n<span class=\"paragraph\">A longitudinal photon term contains <marimo-tex class=\"arithmatex\">||(q^\\mu q^\\nu/(q^2)^2||)</marimo-tex>.\nIts numerator is polynomial and its edge gets <code>edge_powers={edge_id: 2}</code>.\nThe Feynman-gauge term keeps power one. Both use the shared UV expansion.</span>\n<span class=\"paragraph\">To match the reference's <strong>selective infrared rearrangement</strong>, freeze the\nmassive denominators into family coordinates, then discard explicit\npowers of the auxiliary mass in the remaining numerator. The family\nstill contains <marimo-tex class=\"arithmatex\">||(k^2-M||)</marimo-tex>. This omits the compensating mass terms that the\ncomplete UV expansion normally retains; the auxiliary counterterm below\ntherefore differs from the complete-expansion one-loop example.</span>\n<span class=\"paragraph\">Native IBP supplies rational coefficients. Analytic master integrals are\nexplicit inputs: <marimo-tex class=\"arithmatex\">||(A=M[1/\\epsilon+1-\\log M]+O(\\epsilon)||)</marimo-tex> and\n<marimo-tex class=\"arithmatex\">||(V=M[3/(2\\epsilon^2)+(9/2-3\\log M)/\\epsilon]+O(1)||)</marimo-tex>.\nThe two-loop master coefficients are checked to be regular at <marimo-tex class=\"arithmatex\">||(D=4||)</marimo-tex>.\nThe remaining loop-measure factor is <marimo-tex class=\"arithmatex\">||((4\\pi)^{L\\epsilon}||)</marimo-tex>.</span></span>"
          }
        }
      ],
      "console": []
    },
    {
      "id": "lEQa",
